            <ArticleTitle>Oncologic Outcomes of Partial Nephrectomy for Stage T3a Renal Cell Cancer.</ArticleTitle>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1558-7673(17)30332-4</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.clgc.2017.10.016</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Partial nephrectomy (PN) for clinical stage T3 tumors is controversial. Radical nephrectomy (RN) has been associated with a greater rate of chronic kidney disease, an increased risk of cardiovascular disease, and increased mortality compared with PN. We present our long-term 2-center experience with PN for stage pT3a tumors and compare the oncologic outcomes with those of similar patients treated with RN.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MATERIALS AND METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">We reviewed the data from all patients who had undergone nephrectomy for renal cell carcinoma from 1987 to 2015 in 2 medical centers. The study included 134 patients with pathologic stage T3a tumors, of whom 48 and 86 underwent PN and RN, respectively. We compared the 2 groups (PN and RN) using univariate and multivariate analyses.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The tumors of all patients with pathologic stage T3a who had undergone PN had been pathologically upstaged from clinical stage T1 or T2. Univariate and multivariate analyses revealed tumor size was significantly different statistically between the study groups (median, 7.0 cm in RN group vs. 4.0 cm in PN group; P &lt; .001). Surgery type was not a predictor of local recurrence (P = .978), metastatic progression (P = .972), death from renal cancer (P = .626), or death from all causes (P = .974) at the 5-year follow-up point.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The results of the present study have shown similar oncologic outcomes between 48 patients with stage pT3a renal cancer who underwent PN and 86 patients who underwent RN. Although PN was not performed on clinical T3a tumors, our findings suggest that PN can also be considered for these tumors and, thus, avoid the long-term complications of RN. However, strict follow-up protocols are mandatory.</AbstractText>
